@import com.roysolberg.java.hdlserver.Page
@param page: Page
@template.layout(page = page, content = @`
<h1 class="title">Security</h1>
<div class="content box">
    <p><strong>Use HTTPS</strong> for this server if you can. Without it, someone between IFTTT and you could pick up the commands being sent,
        send them again, and maybe guess how to send others.</p>
    <p><strong>Why is there no login on these pages?</strong> Anyone on the network the HDL Buspro bus is on doesn't need this server:
        the bus has no security of its own, and everything on it is sent in plain text. A login would only give a false sense of security.
        So these pages (not the API the commands use) only answer on the local network, or on the same
        <a href="https://tailscale.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Tailscale</a> network, so you can use them away from home too.</p>
    <p><strong>The API and the commands</strong> are guarded by a random key, made the first time the server started.
        Requests without the right key aren't run.</p>
    <div class="notification is-warning is-light">
        <strong>Use at your own risk.</strong> Busbridge is provided as is, without warranty of any kind (MIT license).
        You decide how it's reachable, and you're responsible for that. Like all software, it and the libraries it builds on will
        have weaknesses found over time, so keep it updated: run <code>./update.sh</code>, or turn on <a href="/settings">Check for updates</a>
        to be told when there's a new version. And remember that anyone on your home network can control HDL Buspro directly, with or
        without Busbridge.
    </div>
    <p>There could also be weaknesses in the computer it runs on, your router, other network equipment, IFTTT or Google Assistant.
        If you want to stay completely safe, stay offline.</p>
    <p>Read more about security and HDL Buspro in
        <a href="https://blog.roysolberg.com/2017/08/my-dumb-smart-home" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">My dumb smart home</a>.</p>
</div>
`)
